+# OEIS A284005: Kurkov's lower-bit recurrence
+
+## Verified locator
+
+Source: https://oeis.org/A284005, FORMULA, Mikhail Kurkov's
+November 10, 2019 conjecture. Entry history:
+https://oeis.org/history?seq=A284005. The entry is authored by Antti
+Karttunen; this conjecture is credited to Mikhail Kurkov. OEIS source
+content and its adapted statement here are attributed to these authors
+and the OEIS contributors under CC-BY-SA-4.0:
+https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/.
+
+The NAME states, verbatim:
+
+> a(0) = 1, and for n > 1, a(n) = (1 + A000120(n))*a(floor(n/2)); also a(n) = A000005(A283477(n)).
+
+The entry's initial data supplies a(1)=2. A000120(n) is the number of
+ones in the binary expansion of n. The divisor-count representation in
+the NAME quotation is not a premise or conclusion of the native result.
+
+## Exact question and native result
+
+In normalized notation, for every integer n>1 set L=floor(log_2(n)),
+f(n)=n-2^L and T(n,k)=floor(n/2^k) mod 2. The conjecture is
+
+```text
+a(n) = 2*a(f(n)) + Sum_{k=0..L-1} a(f(n)+2^k*(1-T(n,k))).
+```
+
+Every k from zero through L-1 is included. An existing one bit has
+T(n,k)=1 and contributes a(f(n)); such repeated contributions remain
+in the sum. The domain includes n=2 and all powers of two. It requires
+no value of log_2(0) and asserts no lower-bit identity at n=0 or n=1.
+
+The repository's native result uses the imported A284005 function and
+binary digit sum, and contains the initial values, the defining recurrence
+for every natural n>1, and the complete identity above. Natural-number
+quantification represents exactly the source's integer domain n>1.
+The source supplies the question, not the repository proof. The sequence
+supplier's April 24, 2023 two-parameter binomial theorem is a distinct
+identity and is not used as a substitute for this question.
+
+## Literature scope
+
+The bounded original-entry and history reading, author attribution,
+repository issue/comment reading, and available resolution searches
+identified no exact prior proof in the inspected scope. This establishes
+neither worldwide openness nor priority. OpenAlex rate limiting and
+unavailable StackExchange advanced-search responses leave those surfaces
+unverified. GitHub code-search authorization was unavailable, so no
+complete content-level anonymous-equivalent theorem screen is claimed.
+No DOI or prior proof is attributed to this conjecture.
+
+The OEIS text endpoint reading on 2026-10-28 independently confirms the
+November 10, 2019 FORMULA, its author, the full summation range, and
+the initial data. That line still carries the label "Conjecture".
+The exact question is recorded in
+`Problems/oeis-a284005-kurkov-lower-bit-recurrence.md`; the native result
+is `D5/S1/Digit/KurkovLowerBitRecurrence.result`. The label is a source
+observation, not evidence that no other proof exists.
